use console.println

/// A program the VM cannot run, run the ordinary way.
///
/// ADR 0019's no-silent-fallback rule is what this case pins: a run that
/// names a construct the lowering does not cover fails before anything
/// happens, naming the construct and pointing at it, rather than quietly
/// finishing on the interpreter. The `println` below never runs, and the
/// empty `expected.out` beside this file is how the case says so.
///
/// The construct is a function declared inside a function body. It was a
/// closure until closures lowered, a task scope until task scopes did, and a
/// call whose labelled arguments stand out of declaration order until
/// ADR 0021 made that a check-time error — which is why it had to move again:
/// a program `cove check` refuses never reaches a backend at all, so it can
/// no longer pin what a backend does with one. This construct is unsupported
/// in the plain sense. The interpreter runs it, the lowering has no
/// instruction for it yet, and nothing about the program is wrong.
/// Whichever construct stands here, the point of the case is the rule and not
/// the construct.
///
/// The `command` file names no backend, because since ADR 0022 there is
/// nothing to name: the VM is what `cove run` runs a program on, so this is
/// what a person who wrote this program and typed `cove run` gets.
/// `backend_ast` is the other half — the same program, on the interpreter
/// the help text points at, finishing.
export fn main() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  println("this line never runs")?
  fn double(n: Int) -> Int {
    n * 2
  }
  println("doubled {double(21)}")?
  Ok(())
}
